From: Zurker <91011862+hershate@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 03:06:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Papo: batch 148 (R8) compact uniform representation for
 SWMRNibbleArray - re-opening the batch-144 verdict on two changed grounds:
 the reclaimable population doubled in relative terms (uniform nibbles 22.0MB
 of the re-baselined 831.6MB heap = 2.65%, vs 15.8MB/1.8% at the time of the
 veto) and a full audit of the 441-line class shows the surgery is contained -
 both storage fields are private with every read/write routed through class
 methods, so the concurrent-read protocol is untouched. A nibble whose whole
 4096-nibble payload is 0 or 15 (the two constants the engines produce at
 scale: full-bright sky sections above the heightmap, block-light sections
 that settle to zero) now keeps BOTH storage arrays null and carries the value
 in uniform fields that follow the same SWMR discipline (writer-owned plain
 updatingUniform, volatile visibleUniform; invariant: uniform != 0 => storage
 == null && state in {INIT, HIDDEN}). Value-equivalence is provable at every
 seam: readers resolve the uniform (0 for UNINIT/NULL through the same
 expression by invariant); setFull/setZero flip the uniform without allocation
 on clean null storage (a real value change marks dirty so publication cannot
 be skipped when the state field alone is unchanged); first writes materialise
 AROUND the uniform (swapUpdatingAndMarkDirty fills updatingUniform, so a
 full-bright section keeps its 15s when a propagation edge writes one nibble);
 publish deflates settled arrays (isAllZero/isAllFull scan is cheaper than the
 2048-byte copy it can replace, and frees the arrays to the existing pool);
 extrudeLower resolves uniform sources to setFull/setZero (the caller runs
 setNonNull first, making the state choreography equal to the array path);
 vanilla conversion and the save path MATERIALISE uniform back, so the on-disk
 format and cross-version world compat are byte-identical; disk loads
 canonicalise all-full payloads back to uniform in both the SaveUtil and
 fromVanilla constructor shapes (all-zero payloads keep today's materialised
 form so INIT-with-zero and UNINIT stay engine-distinct). Expected effect: the
 10,737 uniform byte[2048] arrays (22.0MB) in the soak census collapse to
 zero; per-nibble cost is one byte of uniform state. Coverage:
 PapoSWMRUniformNibbleTest (uniform-until-written +
 materialise-around-uniform, flip publication without state change,
 uniform-zero save semantics, save format materialisation proof, load
 canonicalisation + write-through, extrude from
 uniform-full/UNINIT/uniform-zero sources, hidden uniform round-trip, vanilla
 conversion materialisation, publish deflation of settled arrays, and a
 200-round x 500-op randomized differential against a materialised-array
 mirror model across set/setFull/setZero/updateVisible/read) Two
 writer-protocol holes the randomized differential caught before ship: a
 uniform flip leaves updatingDirty set with null storage, so set() (which only
 materialised when clean) and extrudeLower's dirty-reuse branch (into =
 storageUpdating = null) would write through a null array - both now
 materialise whenever storage is absent (swapUpdatingAndMarkDirty early-return
 relaxed to dirty-with-non-null-storage; extrudeLower's materialise branch
 keeps the flip state's INIT/HIDDEN instead of forcing INIT). Coverage
 extended: set-after-unpublished-flip (full and zero flips) +
 extrude-into-flip tests added (PapoSWMRUniformNibbleTest now 11 tests).


